us on getting that list right. the foundational utilities would likely have lower numbers. Since I can't determine the exact PRD numbers without reading the files, I'll use glob patterns in the required_reading section and trust that the stitch agent will resolve them correctly when executing the task.\n\nNow I'm noticing something important in the specstatus field—format shows \"implemented\" while sys shows \"spec_complete\"—and I need to understand what that distinction means for which task to prioritize.\n\nLooking at the definitions more carefully, I realize specstatus refers to whether the specification itself is complete, not whether code exists. Both uc002 and uc003 have finished specs but no code generation yet, so the real difference is that sys is more foundational as an OS-level primitive, which makes it the better choice to propose.\n\nActually, I need to stop second-guessing myself. Both use cases have complete specs and no existing code. Since they're equal in priority and neither depends on the other, I'll go with pkg/format because it comes first alphabetically. It handles column alignment for tabular output and ANSI color codes for file types—straightforward library functionality that doesn't require sys as a dependency.\n\nNow I'm defining the task structure with the specific capabilities: column alignment, color codes, and human-readable size formatting. These three features fit comfortably within the implementation scope, and I'm laying out the YAML structure that references the architecture docs, use case spec, and product requirements while detailing the files that need to be created. I need to keep the package isolated from OS dependencies and name each file after its primary exported type as specified in the PRD, then verify the build passes linting and tests with proper documentation on all public APIs.\n\nLooking at the architecture, the format package provides column alignment, ANSI color codes, and human-readable sizes—so I should name the files after those primary types once I confirm the exact names from the PRD rather than guessing them upfront. I'm realizing the implementation_status in ARCHITECTURE.yaml might be outdated, but the PRD files do exist on disk. So I'll set up required_reading to point to the architecture document, the relevant use-case spec, and include a note with glob notation to locate the PRD for pkg/format so the agent can find and read it before starting.\n\nActually, I should step back—the stitch agent has filesystem access, so I just need to direct it to search for the PRD rather than guessing the exact filename. And I should also include a test file in the files list, since the planning constitution emphasizes splitting implementation across test and implementation files.\n\nBut wait, that creates a problem with the one-task limit. The constitution says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, decomposing features into types, implementation, and tests in that order. With only one task allowed, I can only tackle one of these phases. The logical first step would be the implementation itself, but that means either skipping tests or combining them, which violates the principle.\n\nLet me reconsider what fits in a single task—types and implementation could reasonably go together since the types are minimal, leaving tests for a follow-up task. I'm realizing the acceptance criteria needs adjustment—if tests aren't part of this task, then \"go test passes\" doesn't make sense as a requirement. I could either include a trivial test file despite P2's guidance, or accept that the test command might return \"[no test files]\" and that's acceptable verification that the code at least compiles and is importable.\n\nActually, I'm overthinking this. Since P2 explicitly forbids a single task from both implementing and testing the same feature, I should remove the test execution criterion from this task's acceptance criteria and save testing for a follow-up task. Let me finalize the task definition now.\n\nFor the file structure, I need to figure out the primary type names for each capability. Without the PRD, I'll infer reasonable names based on the three capabilities: column alignment, ANSI colors, and size formatting. With the 250-line limit and roughly 50-80 lines per capability, I should be able to fit everything across 2-3 files while following P7's naming convention that avoids generic package-named files. I'm finishing out the requirements—the second one covers ANSI color helpers for file types and permissions, and there should be more requirements for the size formatting and any validation or edge cases the PRD specifies.\n\nLooking at the design decisions, I need to keep pkg/format isolated from OS-specific imports and follow the naming convention where files match their primary exported types. The acceptance criteria is straightforward: the package needs to build cleanly on both darwin/arm64 and linux/arm without errors.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's an issue with how I specified the required reading—I listed a directory path instead of an actual file, which won't work for the stitch agent. I need to point to the specific PRD file rather than the directory containing it, since the format expects individual file paths with optional reasons. I'll finalize the task specification with the required reading paths and file structure for the format package implementation.\n\nThe three core modules need to be created: a table alignment module for columnar output, a color module with ANSI constants for file types and permissions, and a size formatting module for converting bytes to human-readable units. These map directly to the three main requirements that drive the implementation.\n\nI need to be careful about dependencies—pkg/format can't import from pkg/sys or external packages, only the standard library. I should also verify the exact type names in the PRD before finalizing the file names, since those are just placeholders right now. The acceptance criteria are straightforward: the code needs to build cleanly, pass linting, and have proper documentation on all exported symbols.\n\nNow I'm verifying that all three core features—column alignment, ANSI color support, and size formatting—are properly exposed as public APIs matching the PRD specification.\n\nLet me check this against the constraints: the task stays within release 00.0, doesn't duplicate the existing magefiles issue, and includes all required sections with proper field structure. I've got three requirements, four acceptance criteria, and two design decisions, which fits the guidelines. The estimated implementation scope of 200-250 lines seems reasonable for these formatting utilities.\n\nThe main limitation is that I can't anchor the PRD requirements more precisely without seeing the actual PRD document, but the stitch agent will have access to that when implementing. One thing I should reconsider is the note in the files section about renaming—that's a bit unconventional, and I need to think through whether that aligns with the planning constitution around Go package naming conventions. I'm laying out the task structure with three files to create: table.go for column alignment, color.go for ANSI color handling, and a size.go file (implied by the pattern), along with the required reading from architecture and specification documents to guide the implementation. I'm confirming the naming convention for source files—each should be named after its primary exported type as per project standards, and I need to verify the canonical type names in the PRD before creating anything. The acceptance criteria are clear: the build must succeed, linting must pass, all exports need documentation, and the three core capabilities—column alignment, ANSI colors, and size formatting—must be fully implemented as public APIs matching the spec.\n\nNow I'm doing a final review of the task structure. Everything checks out: it's scoped to the right release and use case, there's no duplicate issue, it's a single focused task within the line budget, all required fields are present, and the design decisions properly reference both the architecture and project conventions. The one thing I should tighten up is the required_reading path—instead of pointing to the entire product-requirements directory, I need to be more specific and ask the agent to find the actual PRD file for this feature.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19116,"cache_read_input_tokens":15967,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19116},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91c8bb78-e232-4ce8-945d-0646b91d120b","uuid":"6c932ca5-0118-4dc3-ad37-0e061e128585"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Fe3V7gTfPNDzegPkQh7w3Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Step 1–2: Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils experiments with specification-driven code generation: derive formal specs from C source (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils), generate Go implementations via mage-claude-orchestrator, and verify behavioral parity through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- Human operator: writes PRDs and use cases from C source analysis\n- `docs/specs/`: specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites) — human-authored, pipeline reads but never writes\n- `cmd/<utility>/`: per-utility Go implementations + differential tests\n- `pkg/sys/`: Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions (golang.org/x/sys)\n- `pkg/format/`: output formatting (tables, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `pkg/testutils/`: differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/`: mage targets wrapping the orchestrator (build, lint, analyze, stitch)\n\n**Implementation status**: Working branch is specs-only. Generated code (pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/ts, cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge) lives at generation-merged tags from a prior pipeline run. No Go code on the current branch.\n\n**Current release**: `00.0` — Batch 0 foundational infrastructure.\n- uc001-magefiles: issue #290 exists (status: ready) — already tracked\n- uc002-format: spec_complete, no issue yet\n- uc003-sys: spec_complete, no issue yet\n- uc004-testutils: spec_complete, no issue yet\n\n## Step 3: Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 00.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Issue #290 covers uc001. The three remaining use cases have no issues.\n\nCanonical ordering (libraries before consumers; alphabetical tiebreaker):\n1. `pkg/format` (no dependencies on pkg/sys or pkg/testutils)\n2. `pkg/sys` (no dependencies on other pkg/ packages)\n3. `pkg/testutils` (may depend on pkg/sys)\n\n`pkg/format` < `pkg/sys` alphabetically → **propose pkg/format first**.\n\nPer P2 (implementation and tests must be separate tasks), this task covers implementation only; tests are a follow-up.\n\n## Step 4: Task Proposal\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"pkg/format: implement column alignment, ANSI color, and size formatting (rel00.0-uc002)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/format component section -- capabilities and references)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc002-format.yaml (use case scope and success criteria)\n      - \"docs/specs/product-requirements/ (use Glob to find prd*-format.yaml; read it fully before writing any code)\"\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/table.go\n        action: create\n        note: column alignment for tabular output; rename to match primary exported type in PRD per project convention\n      - path: pkg/format/color.go\n        action: create\n        note: ANSI color constants and helpers for file types and Unix permission strings\n      - path: pkg/format/size.go\n        action: create\n        note: human-readable size conversion from raw byte counts to K/M/G/T suffixes\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: Implement column-alignment API for tabular output per the pkg/format PRD, producing aligned multi-column output from variable-width string fields (ls -l style)\n      - id: R2\n        text: Implement ANSI color constants and helper functions for file types and Unix permission strings per the pkg/format PRD\n      - id: R3\n        text: Implement human-readable size formatting that converts raw byte counts to K/M/G/T suffixes using the rounding and threshold rules specified in the pkg/format PRD\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: pkg/format must not import pkg/sys, golang.org/x/sys, or any package outside the Go standard library; it is a pure formatting library with no OS or syscall dependencies\n      - id: D2\n        text: Name each source file after its primary exported type, not after the package (project convention P7); read the PRD to confirm canonical type names before creating files -- the paths in the files list are placeholders if the PRD specifies different type names\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: go build ./pkg/format/... succeeds with no errors\n      - id: AC2\n        text: golangci-lint passes with no warnings on all files under pkg/format/\n      - id: AC3\n        text: All exported functions, types, and constants in pkg/format/ have Go doc comments\n      - id: AC4\n        text: All three capabilities -- column alignment, ANSI color, and size formatting -- are implemented as exported APIs that match the pkg/format PRD requirements\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19116,"cache_read_input_tokens":15967,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19116},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91c8bb78-e232-4ce8-945d-0646b91d120b","uuid":"5663dae9-f393-4393-8f7e-17aa7af7a136"}
